-2.1 By [F5] under [A1], the completion maps $A\to T$ and $B\to T$ are faithfully flat. For a nonzero $a\in A$, the ideal $(a)$ is a nonzero $A$-module. Faithfulness means $(a)\otimes_A T\ne0$: otherwise the nonexact sequence $0\to(a)\to0$ would become exact after tensoring. Flatness makes the multiplication map $(a)\otimes_A T\to A\otimes_A T=T$ injective. If $a$ mapped to zero in $T$, this map would be zero, a contradiction. Hence $A\to T$ is injective. If an element of $A$ maps to zero in $B$, its image in $T$ is zero as well, so injectivity of $A\to T$ makes it zero. Therefore $A\to B$ is injective. [A1, F5, F6, step 1.2, algebra]
